Americans are finally getting into train travel

There=E2=80=99s something decidedly romantic about trains. They=E2=80=99re =
generally pretty comfy, and you can walk to a dining car or the one designe=
d for scenic vistas. There=E2=80=99s also, at least according to our sugges=
ted Netflix titles, a chance there might be a murder mystery afoot.

Americans =E2=80=94 despite living in a nation that has long prioritized dr=
iving while others invested in rail =E2=80=94 are increasingly choosing to =
travel via train.

Amtrak logged 34.5m customer trips and $2.7B in adjusted ticket revenue in =
FY 2025, a 10% increase from FY 2024, per (https://www.businessinsider.com/=
amtrak-booming-why-more-americans-taking-trains-record-ridership-revenue-20=
26-1?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8zV3GEX1Co1_BKWIz674Kxbj00I9_NPxSCtGy2--7LOBYsQIvezFR=
f-V916913WBuXtYE73COoIkLa7Ilhu86Zv9neLw&_hsmi=3D398640795 ) Business Inside=
r.

Why?

Trains not only rule for the aforementioned reasons, but they=E2=80=99ve al=
so been getting better.

Amtrak, which is largely owned by the US government, has made a number of i=
mprovements in recent years to both its trains and stations, helping to cur=
b delays =E2=80=94 especially on its shorter routes, which are the ones pas=
sengers use most often.

At the same time, other forms of transit kinda suck.

Amtrak travelers can read a book, catch up on work, or do a crossword puzzl=
e, whereas drivers are stuck paying constant attention and gritting their t=
eeth through gridlock.

Then there=E2=80=99s flying. Driven in part by factors like labor shortages=
 and the rising cost of jet fuel, airline fares were up 25% (https://www.cn=
bc.com/select/airline-ticket-prices-are-up-25-percent-why-and-how-to-save/?=
_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8zV3GEX1Co1_BKWIz674Kxbj00I9_NPxSCtGy2--7LOBYsQIvezFRf-V91=
6913WBuXtYE73COoIkLa7Ilhu86Zv9neLw&_hsmi=3D398640795 ) YoY in June 2025, th=
e highest spike since tracking began in 1989.

And that extra cash is often funding a demonstrably worse experience:

-

Seats have gotten smaller (https://dailypassport.com/why-airlines-are-shrin=
king-legroom/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8zV3GEX1Co1_BKWIz674Kxbj00I9_NPxSCtGy2--7LOB=
YsQIvezFRf-V916913WBuXtYE73COoIkLa7Ilhu86Zv9neLw&_hsmi=3D398640795 ) , both=
 slimmer and closer together, with less padding.

-

Perks are disappearing (https://www.thestreet.com/travel/american-united-an=
d-delta-air-lines-quietly-end-popular-flight-perk?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8zV3GEX1=
Co1_BKWIz674Kxbj00I9_NPxSCtGy2--7LOBYsQIvezFRf-V916913WBuXtYE73COoIkLa7Ilhu=
86Zv9neLw&_hsmi=3D398640795 ) . Southwest Airlines ditched its famous =E2=
=80=9Cbags fly free=E2=80=9D policy, while other airlines no longer offer f=
ree upgrades for loyalty program members.

-

Aging technology and a lack of air traffic controllers have led to widespre=
ad disturbances (https://thehustle.co/news/why-air-travel-is-so-chaotic-rig=
ht-now?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8zV3GEX1Co1_BKWIz674Kxbj00I9_NPxSCtGy2--7LOBYsQIvez=
FRf-V916913WBuXtYE73COoIkLa7Ilhu86Zv9neLw&_hsmi=3D398640795#:~:text=3DMeanw=
hile%2C%20aging%20technology%20has%20caused,Travel%20And%20Leisure ) , many=
 of which made national news and likely diminished confidence in air travel=
.

Not to mention, TSA has long been a hassle, with far more baggage restricti=
ons than Amtrak, and a more laborious security screening process. Amtrak pa=
ssengers can generally show up ~20 minutes before their train leaves, grab =
a coffee, and hop on.

